Matthew what drew you to this project  how did you become involved with Mexican  cartels and vigilante groups so I first  I first heard about the story through  some articles that I read I started  filming in Arizona I was there for about  four or five months and then my father  actually sent me an article about the  autodefense s in Michoacan and right  when I read it I knew I wanted to create  this parallel story of vigilantism on  both sides of the border so you started  with the Arizona people how did you get  involved with the group why did they  learn to trust you  you know I think it's actually much  harder to get access to the Arizona  vigilantes took me about four or five  months through a lot of phone calls and  sort of pre-production to gain their  trust and and then eventually once I  stepped foot down they're sort of you  know whereas in Mexico I reached out to  the doctor  I got a read about him in article I read  a Wall Street Journal article and and  then reached out through the journalist  and I got on the phone with him the next  day and he said I told him I want to  come down I want to document your story  oh and I commend the story of the outer  defenses and two weeks later I was down  there filming so tell us about your  first impressions of him he certainly  seemed to be someone who loved the  camera you know he's a complex guy and I  think you know one of my goals with this  film was the sort of exam and what  pushes what drives men and women to take  up arms to take the law into their own  hands you know I constantly have sort of  wondered well you know what would I do  if I was in that situation what would I  do if my sister was raped my brother was  was hanging from a bridge you know would  I take up arms but I fight violence with  violence is that right that just is  vigilantism sustainable and then I think  clearly as we see how the film plays out  we end up with a much different story  than I ever could have imagined so as  you were shooting this you watched your  own  compass begin to change as ever enacted  for vigilantism to be enough but IIIi  you don't want to examine what provoked  other people do I mean I wasn't about to  join their better defenses but you know  it's also hard to judge unless you're in  that situation so I really wanted to go  down you know they've been a lot of  stories about narco violence about you  know the drug wars and I really you know  I didn't want to make a policy film I  don't want to make a film with Talking  Heads I want to make an experiential  film in which I put myself and my crew  right in the middle of the action and  when I first started especially in  Mexico I thought it was a very simple  hero villain story of guys in white  shirts fighting against guys and black  shirts and over time that story changed  and the lines between good and evil  became much more blurry let's talk about  time how long were you actually down  there embedded with them I was down  there for about in Mexico for about nine  months one to two weeks of every month  and I actually think that's what allowed  me to get into the sort of dark corners  that I was able to get into you know  there's there other people down there  covering the story you know but they  often will go down for one day two days  three days four days it's so difficult  to tell its story to tell a new story in  that amount of time and you know I had  the fortune to be able to be there for  so long to develop relationships to  develop storylines you know over a  nine-month period did you see those  storylines begin to change in ways that  you had never anticipated completely I  mean I I really ended up with a much  much different story than I then I  started with and I'm not a war reporter  I've never been in situations like this  before and obviously you know as you saw  in the film I found myself and you know  meth labs shootouts torture chambers  places that you know were frightening  and I and I never sort of could dream  that I'd be in those situations but you  know that's where the story led me can  you take us into what it was like as a  filmmaker to ink  after these kind of dangerous situations  were you ever fearful for your life I  think I'd be crazy or inhuman if I  didn't fear of my life there's many many  you know fighting moments that first  shootout you know was particularly  frightening frightening you know every  single day we wore bulletproof vests but  that particular day was it was really  really hot and nothing was going on we  were at a car store one of the other  defensive commanders was gonna buy a car  and so it seemed like nothing was going  on where based I don't like a you know  lunch break  grab my lunch and then BOOM something to  get this call that Janecka and caballo  those two assassins were in a warehouse  we jump in the car and then you know  suddenly were in the middle of a  shootout and that was the first time  that I ever was in a situation like that  before and actually think that the craft  of filmmaking being behind the camera  focusing on on focusing on exposure on  framing you know making sure the record  button was on so helped calm me down in  those situations but but I think I will  say though that despite you know the  adrenaline moments the scariest moment  for me by far was an interview that I  did with the woman Milagros whose  husband was kidnapped by the cartel and  was chopped up to pieces in front of her  and and then burned to death and to be  in this room with this woman and to look  there and to sort of see these deeply  hollow eyes almost as if her soul had  been sucked out of her to hear describe  these horrors and to think that were the  same species of human beings that would  do that to other people that sort of  mentally stuck with me much more so than  any of those other moments as the film  progresses in Mexico of course we  discover the government's involvement  and the kind of fluid boundaries between  the cartel and the government was this  something from reading that you knew was  down the line the line in a sense  you know I think part of what the story  is is is what happens when government  institutions fail what happens when  people live in a society where they have  no one to turn to and a lawless society  I mean that young woman in the beginning  of the film in this scene when they kick  out the army and she's screaming at the  at the policeman her whole family was  killed by the cartel her parents I think  her dad was maybe like seven years old  he was on a respirator they kidnapped  him kidnapped his her mom and her two  brothers and and and murdered them and  she couldn't go to the police we were  one of the first people she ever told  and to live in a society where you know  the very institutions that are there to  protect you either aren't there or if  they are there they're in collusion with  the cartel I mean it's a it's a scary  world and that's where this movement was  born out of unfortunately you know the  movement was changed and and co-opted  and evolved in jail or dr. Morales is  still in jail it's almost just a year  since he's been in jail there's been no  due process no trial you know his family  and his supporters believe that he is in  jail to silence him and that until the  government wants to hear from him that  it will remain in jail so at its heart  are these two men he's two very very  complex men they're both 55 years old al  doctor and Naylor they both believe that  the government has failed them they both  taken the law in their own hands to to  fight for what they believe in but the  circumstances are quite different  you know Mexico the suffering is you  know amazing unbearable 80,000 plus  people killed since 2007 20,000 plus  people disappeared since 2007 whereas in  Arizona obviously that violence is not  happening  it'sit's silly more theoretical to fear  that the Mexican drug wars will seep its  way across their border so you know  obviously they're they're quite  different the stories  you know held together by a sort of  distrust of the you know institutions to  protect us and a desire to bring take  the law into their own hands
 

